{"name":"Acapulco Dive","hex":"#65a7dd","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"dive","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To swim under water.","To jump into water head-first."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A jump or plunge into water.","A headfirst jump toward the ground or into another substance."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dive"}],"entries":[{"title":"Acapulco","blocks":[{"text":"Acapulco de Juárez, commonly called Acapulco, is a city and major seaport in the state of Guerrero on the Pacific coast of Mexico, 380 kilometres (240 mi) south of Mexico City. Located on a deep, semicircular bay, Acapulco has been a port since the early colonial period in Mexico's history. It is a port of call for shipping and cruise lines running between Panama and San Francisco, California. The city of Acapulco is the largest in the state, larger than the state capital Chilpancingo. Acapulco is also Mexico's largest beach and balneario resort city. Acapulco de Juárez, Guerrero is the municipal seat of the municipality of Acapulco, Guerrero."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Skyscrapers_in_Acapulco_Diamante.jpg/330px-Skyscrapers_in_Acapulco_Diamante.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acapulco","wikidata":"Q81398"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Acapulco3.jpg/500px-Acapulco3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Acapulco3.jpg/1280px-Acapulco3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Acapulco3.jpg/500px-Acapulco3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAcapulco3.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikime